 true] [17592186124483 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399419 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124483 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399426 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124484 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399433 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124484 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399440 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124486 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399482 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124550 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399622 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399608 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399615 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126392 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399629 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129487 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(3i + 2j)m/s^{2}$. The velocity of the particle is $(-7i - 6j)m/s$ when $t = 0s$. Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $2im/s$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129487 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(3i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(-7i-6j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $2i\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129487 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=3i+2j,\\,u=-7i-6j,\\,v=2i\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-7i-6j)+(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =3ti+2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9=3t \\\\ 6=2t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399454 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399461 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399447 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399370 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399384 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161973 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399363 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186179660 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399587 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229143 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399356 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399357 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1069 "q-130261" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2xy$\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+xy$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1072 #uuid "3d8c3559-a0d3-57b0-8496-a5eb629e4fde" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2ax{y}_{1}-2bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{1}]+b[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =a[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}]+b[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 175921863