es of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$ is strictly decreasing." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399559 1072 #uuid "0ca3eeff-b7f5-5d53-aae2-987010ec68f9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399559 1074 "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{2}+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{2}+1}<0.\n$$\n\nThe function $2{x}^{2}+1$ is always positive. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\n4x<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399560 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399561 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399560 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399562 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399560 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399563 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399560 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399564 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399560 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399565 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399560 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399560 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399560 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399565 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399560 1080 #uuid "43f397fc-f0b4-510a-8cdc-2eadab74ebfa" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399561 1081 "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399561 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399561 1084 #uuid "60d7c758-6513-5b62-9838-0f0e3e990c96" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399562 1081 "x\\in (\\sqrt{2},\\infty )" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399562 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399562 1084 #uuid "79246e8a-df03-50c3-a1b1-e08f5dedc89a" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399563 1081 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399563 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399563 1084 #uuid "4af42b3a-0a5c-585b-b625-a30cb6ff9720" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399564 1081 "x\\in (-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399564 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399564 1084 #uuid "7cc9b135-ebc1-5040-b029-e3841fd75c3d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399565 1081 "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399565 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399565 1084 #uuid "65da45d5-5844-595b-8bdf-71df5cfdf77e" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399567 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399566 1069 "q-132852" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399566 1071 "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years.\nAdditionally, $3%$ of batteries last less than $1.75$ years. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399566 1072 #uuid "42e998b4-da64-533d-80e4-6303495d71cd" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399566 1074 "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<1.75)=0.03.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.75) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)=-1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.8808 \\\\ 1.75-\\mu  & =-0.9404 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈2.69.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399567 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399568 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399567 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399569 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399567 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399570 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399567 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399571 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399567 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399572 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399567 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399567 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399567 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399568 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399567 1080 #uuid "eb566678-fdf6-552d-aad6-4bf9b9328ba8" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399568 1081 "$2.69$ years" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399568 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399568 1084 #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399569 1081 "$2.74$ years" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399569 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399569 1084 #uuid "f4c15084-f362-505f-8950-6df3b2548ace" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399570 1081 "$2.65$ years" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399570 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399570 1084 #uuid "e01f491c-202f-5132-a3f5-3f0f2930aa15" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399571 1081 "$2.57$ years" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399571 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399571 1084 #uuid "79ea6bf2-0abb-5dd3-8132-52b2488943a9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399572 1081 "$2.79$ years" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399572 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399572 1084 #uuid "7b383bc1-6fb9-59e8-b057-fb6dccdf74a2" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399574 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399573 1069 "q-132856" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399573 1071 "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.7.$ In addition, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.75\\mu $ and $$1.25\\mu .$ Find the average cinema admission fee, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399573 1072 #uuid "50c01113-7eba-59c9-b302-374966a56f93" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399573 1074 "Let $X$ represent an admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & =1.8808 \\\\ 0.25\\mu  & =1.31656 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.31656}{0.25} \\\\  & ≈5.27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399575 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399576 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399577 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399578 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399579 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399574 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399574 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399574 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399575 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399574 1080 #uuid "27f6174e-7ddd-5db0-b8a0-9648c670a8d3" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399575 1081 "$$5.27$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399575 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399575 1084 #uuid "6028dff8-a045-5c36-b03a-7cc1e9d875d0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399576 1081 "$$3.73$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399576 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399576 1084 #uuid "fbfc67ac-b865-541b-abd3-49ba35181b7e" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399577 1081 "$$4.85$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399577 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399577 1084 #uuid "7922a355-3dcf-5ee9-a956-87ba8d17b65c" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399578 1081 "$$4.41$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399578 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399578 1084 #uuid "be6f8eda-cb13-5113-8a9a-1637beaf9d1e" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399579 1081 "$$5.38$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399579 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399579 1084 #uuid "7ddb9a99-6c2a-5cfd-b39f-b1b603fa424c" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399581 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399580 1069 "q-133221" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399580 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=axy-5{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}-bxy.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399580 1072 #uuid "c7b780a7-788b-5d60-b089-16f56470436d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399580 1074 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(axy-5{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =ax-10y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-bxy) \\\\  & =2x-by\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. H